module gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow

// THE REQUIRED CONTEXT IS ONE JOB (operator ruling 2026-10-09, the v1 withdrawal): "v1 is
// effectively bankrupt due to memory instability - i want to go back to pure v2 native compiler
// development - that means deleting most of our current CI jobs", and, on the first cut of this
// change, "collapse emit-build plus the aggregate into one required job named witnesses, and make
// the job's regeneration claim true: this version requires fresh emission/build of both retained
// products and refuses a restored final-product hit. Ordinary Cargo reuse may remain."
//
// This module emits .github/workflows/witnesses.yml. Until that ruling a `witnesses` aggregate read
// five lanes, and four of them judged through the v1 interpreter or judged v1's own Rust: `floor`
// (the interpreter folding the rostered witnesses), `generated` (the stage0 mirrors, the registry
// drift discriminator with its repair chain, and the lint), `rust-unit-tests`, and `seed` (the
// compiler pair built once for those three). They are DELETED here rather than retuned, because a
// lane made survivable is investment in the implementation the ruling retires. What they held is the
// declared drop gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn, and its trigger names the native
// compiler judging that population, never the return of these lanes.
//
// WHAT REMAINS IS ONE JOB, `v2-bootstrap` (gunbc.required_ci_contract_epoch required_ci_context_name)
// -- the id the repository ruleset requires, so the job's own
// check IS the required context and no aggregate reads it: the seed emits the two retained v2
// closures, cargo builds the emitted crates, and the built binaries are started
// (gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate states the subject). Every run emits and builds both products
// FRESH: the native product store's restore and save steps left this job, and each subject step
// passes native_product_reuse_env_name=fresh, under which the runner refuses a restored hit rather
// than serving it. That is the one use the ruling leaves the seed. A verdict this gate gains from
// here is a row of emitted_subject_build_rows judged by a binary built from that emission, not a
// lane that hands the corpus to the v1 interpreter.
//
// ONE JOB MEANS NO LANE ROSTER. The derived aggregate -- lane rows, standings, the env block and
// shell conditionals folded from them -- existed so that several lanes could block through one
// required context without a hand-written `needs` edge; with one job there is nothing to join, and
// keeping the fold over a one-row roster would be a second representation of the job list (DESIGN
// section 2). A second required job is a roster growth the operator must sign off (the design
// document's CI row); the job that lands it brings the fold back with it.
//
// FORK PULL REQUESTS CARRY NO REQUIRED CONTEXT. The job keeps the fleet guard
// (compiler_gate_fleet_job_condition): fork code does not run on the fleet, so on a fork pull
// request the job is skipped and the required check is never produced -- the head is UNOBSERVED
// and cannot merge until a same-repository branch carries it. The deleted aggregate excused that
// skip with a notice and a green context; a green over no verdict is the fail-open DESIGN section
// 5 refuses, and nothing in this repository lands from a fork.
//
// THE JOB ID IS THE CONTRACT NAME. It was `witnesses` through the v1 withdrawal, which kept the
// spelling while the obligations changed (epoch bump). The name is now `v2-bootstrap`, the
// operator-approved spelling of what the job actually does, and gunbc.required_ci_contract_epoch
// required_ci_context_name is the one authority both the emitter and the ruleset read. The epoch is
// a digest of the obligation rows, so a material change of those obligations cannot keep the old
// identity.
//
// THE REQUIRED JOB RUNS ON THE FLEET (operator ruling 2026-09-28: no lane the required context
// reads runs on a hosted runner), and compiler_gate_merge_path_runs_on_the_fleet refuses emission
// of a workflow in which it runs hosted. The ruling's receipts were merge-queue runs 36478070174 and
// 36464765845, where every hosted job waited minutes for a slot in a pool this repository does not
// control.
//
// SUPERSESSION CANCELS A FLEET JOB. The concurrency policy cancels a running pull-request run on the
// next push. gunbc.compile_permit_scope fleet_compile_cancellation_standing still reads NotSafe
// (producer absent), so a cancelled fleet compile may leak its ctrl-jobserver permits; this module
// does not resolve that. merge_group and heal revalidation keep their immutable groups
// (witness_floor_concurrency_group), so a later push never cancels them.

